# Line endings: LF in the repository and in every checkout, on every OS.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which checks text files out with CRLF. Then the
# working tree differs byte for byte between Windows and macOS: anything that hashes or copies repo
# files (scripts/build_external.py hashes its own sources) disagrees across machines, and shell
# scripts such as .githooks/* stop running. eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf.
#
# After pulling this file into an existing Windows clone, files already checked out keep CRLF until
# they are rewritten. On a clean tree (commit or stash first): git rm -r -q --cached . && git reset --hard
* text=auto eol=lf

# Must be LF whatever a local setting says: sh refuses "#!/bin/sh\r".
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Never convert, never diff as text. Most of these must not be committed at all (see
# scripts/firewall_check.py); marking them binary keeps an accidental add from being rewritten.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.gzip binary
*.7z binary
*.npz binary
*.npy binary
*.parquet binary
*.pkl binary
*.pickle binary
*.joblib binary
*.pt binary
*.pth binary
*.onnx binary
*.safetensors binary
*.mp4 binary
*.pl3container binary
